{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["Scripture states in the section of Leviticus, \"Speak to the children of Israel,\" for there it commands concerning the bringing of the offerings, and Israel brings them. And here it says <b>Command Aaron</b>, for it will speak of the performance of the offerings, and the priests perform them. Rashi wrote (Rashi on Leviticus 6:2): \"צו (command) means nothing other than exhortation, immediately and for all generations.\" Rabbi Shimon said: \"Scripture had all the more need to exhort in a matter involving monetary loss.\" But the midrash of Rabbi Shimon is not about this commandment, for here there is no monetary loss for the sons of Aaron who are commanded in it; rather, they have gain and reward in all the offerings, including the burnt offering. Rather, the first Tanna said: צו means nothing other than exhortation — immediately and for all generations — to say that in the sections where Scripture wishes to exhort and to say that it be done immediately and the matter observed for all generations, it says in them צו, and in the rest of the sections it says \"Speak to the children of Israel\" or \"Say to them.\" And Rabbi Shimon comes to disagree and to say that sometimes this expression occurs in a matter that is not immediate and for all generations, because it involves monetary loss — for example, the commandment stated regarding the oil for lighting, and likewise where Scripture says, \"Command the children of Israel and they shall give the Levites cities to dwell in.\" And it is possible that it is said that there is monetary loss for the priests in this צו, on account of \"this is the offering of Aaron and his sons\" which follows upon this commandment. But at the beginning of Sifrei there is a dispute on this matter, cited there differently:" "<b>This is the law of the burnt offering</b> — this matter comes to teach concerning the burning of fats and limbs, that it is valid all night long, and to teach concerning disqualified offerings, which of them, if they went up, must come down, and which of them, if they went up, do not come down — for every instance of תורת (the law of) expands, one law for all that go up [on the altar], even disqualified ones that if they went up do not come down. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 6:2). But this law is not for all that go up; rather, it is for all burnt offerings, for the libations, if they went up, do come down — and even valid libations where the animal offering is disqualified, or a valid animal offering with disqualified libations, and all the more so if both are disqualified — in all these cases the animal offering does not come down but the libations do come down. This is the expansion of the verse: \"the law of the burnt offering\" — one law for all burnt offerings, and not for libations and not for blood, according to the words of Rabbi Yehoshua (in Torat Kohanim and in Zevaḥim 9a). Now, this verse teaches that we do not offer the burnt offering at night, but if it was slaughtered and its blood was thrown by day, we burn its limbs all night long, and likewise the fats of the peace offerings. And the meaning of \"it is the burnt offering\" is like: the burnt offering shall be upon the burning, upon the altar all night long. And it says היא (it) for the exclusion that they derived from it. And according to the midrash (Torat Kohanim here), Scripture does not teach that we bring the limbs up from the ground onto the altar at night, for they already learned this from another verse (Exodus 23:18), \"the fat of My festival shall not remain until morning\" — for from there they learn (Mekhilta Mishpatim 20:18) that disqualified offerings are subject to the prohibition of remaining [overnight] upon the floor, and they go up [on the altar] all night long. And here it speaks of limbs that are already upon the altar by day, that they turn them over all night long until morning. Therefore it says \"the burnt offering\" — for it is already burnt upon the altar. And it further teaches that even disqualified offerings do not come down, provided their disqualification is in holiness, and that the libations do come down, and likewise the blood, as I have written:" "<b>And the fire of the altar shall burn on it</b> — it says that it shall burn on the altar all night long, for it is a commandment that they place much wood by day, so that [the offerings] not be entirely consumed and the fire go out from it. And in my opinion, what it says (Leviticus 6:6), \"A constant fire shall burn on the altar; it shall not go out\" — this is a commandment to the priests regarding the maintenance of the fire, as it says (Leviticus 6:5), \"And the priest shall kindle wood upon it,\" and He commanded that they be zealous in this and arrange fire and much wood, so that the fire burn constantly all day and all night, and He warned with a negative commandment that it not go out ever. Now, if the priests were negligent and the fire went out, they transgress a negative commandment. Because of this, our Rabbis said (Yoma 45a) that there was a second arrangement of wood for maintaining the fire. And what it says (Leviticus 6:5), \"And the fire on the altar shall burn on it; it shall not go out\" — this is a superfluous verse, and it is derived by our Rabbis (Torat Kohanim, chapter 2:7) to include all people: it teaches that anyone who extinguishes [the fire] transgresses a negative commandment, and even if he extinguished a single ember from the arrangement, he is liable to lashes — whether he extinguished it on top of the altar or whether he took it down and extinguished it below. And it seems to me that he does not transgress except one negative commandment:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The reference to 'at the beginning of Sifrei' likely refers to Sifrei Zuta on Numbers (parashat Naso); the text appears to cite a variant version of the dispute attributed there to Rabbi Shimon."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:57.191264820Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}